.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PLAKAR-NOTION 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm plakar-notion
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
integration package provides source and destination connectors that
back up and restore Notion workspaces through the public API: pages,
databases and their data sources, rows, blocks, files hosted by
Notion, comments and views.
.Pp
It needs an internal connection token with the read content
capability for backups, the insert and update content capabilities
for restores, the comment capabilities for the comments and the user
information capability for the member list.
The pages and databases to back up must be shared with the connection.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration parameters are as follow:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar location
For a source,
.Li notion://
backs up everything shared with the connection and
.Li notion://<id>
a single page or database and what is below it.
For a destination,
.Li notion://<page id>
is the page the backup is recreated under.
.It Ar token
The connection token, starting with
.Li ntn_ .
Defaults to the
.Ev NOTION_TOKEN
environment variable, which keeps it out of the command line.
.It Ar comments
For a source, which comments to back up:
.Li blocks
(every discussion, at the cost of a request per block, the default),
.Li pages
(the page-level discussions only, one request per page) or
.Li none .
For a destination, whether to recreate the comments
(the default).
.It Ar users
Whether to back up the member list.
Defaults to
.Li true .
.It Ar views
Whether to back up or recreate the views of databases.
Defaults to
.Li true .
.It Ar rate
Requests per second sent to Notion.
Defaults to 3, which every plan allows; Business and Enterprise
workspaces allow 10.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following example shows how to configure a source called
.Li mynotion
for a workspace to back up:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ export NOTION_TOKEN=ntn_...
$ plakar source add mynotion notion://
.Ed
.Pp
Supposing that a Kloset store named
.Li mystore
is already configured,
the following command will back up the workspace to this store:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ plakar at @mystore backup @mynotion
.Ed
.Pp
To restore a snapshot under an existing page:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ plakar destination add mynotion-restore notion://<page id>
$ plakar at @mystore restore -to @mynotion-restore <snapshot>
.Ed
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Notion assigns new ids to what it creates: a restore builds a copy
under the target page and never touches the originals, so restoring
twice gives two copies.
A child database and a page that lived inside a toggle or a column are
restored at the end of their page.
Blocks the API cannot create are left out with a warning, link
previews become bookmarks.
Resolved comments are not exposed by the API; restored comments are
written by the connection with the original author as display name.
Views are recreated best effort.
Pages in the trash are not backed up.
